1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,948
When you ask an AI to write SQL, you get a query that looks right.

2
00:00:04,968 --> 00:00:08,561
My project, CheckedSQL, tells you when it isn't.

3
00:00:08,581 --> 00:00:16,390
It takes the AI's query, runs it on a real database, and compares the answer with one I checked by hand.

4
00:00:16,410 --> 00:00:17,843
Why would anyone care?

5
00:00:17,863 --> 00:00:19,693
A wrong query doesn't crash.

6
00:00:19,713 --> 00:00:24,052
It returns a number that looks just as confident as the right one.

7
00:00:24,072 --> 00:00:32,110
CheckedSQL makes those mistakes visible, and it goes one step further: it checks whether the checker itself can be fooled.

8
00:00:32,130 --> 00:00:35,400
Others have built big benchmarks for this.

9
00:00:35,420 --> 00:00:41,587
Spider, from 2018, has about ten thousand questions over two hundred databases.

10
00:00:41,607 --> 00:00:46,756
BIRD, from 2023, has over twelve thousand on larger, messier data.

11
00:00:46,776 --> 00:00:54,980
In BIRD's original paper, ChatGPT got about forty percent right, against ninety-three percent for people.

12
00:00:55,000 --> 00:00:58,921
Researchers also found that the checker can be wrong.

13
00:00:58,941 --> 00:01:10,597
In 2020, Zhong, Yu and Klein showed that running a query on just one database can give a false positive: a wrong query can happen to return the right answer.

14
00:01:10,617 --> 00:01:14,390
Their fix was to test each query on many databases.

15
00:01:14,410 --> 00:01:21,656
There are also tools, like the open-source library Vanna, that let you chat with your database and get SQL back.

16
00:01:21,676 --> 00:01:23,472
They help you write queries.

17
00:01:23,492 --> 00:01:27,170
They don't hand you a record of which answers were wrong.

18
00:01:27,190 --> 00:01:27,974
So why mine?

19
00:01:27,994 --> 00:01:31,527
It isn't a new benchmark, and it isn't more accurate.

20
00:01:31,547 --> 00:01:39,369
It's small enough that one person can read every question, check every answer, and rerun everything with one command.

21
00:01:39,389 --> 00:01:43,190
And it puts the checker's own blind spots in the results.

22
00:01:43,210 --> 00:01:44,797
Here's how I built it.

23
00:01:44,817 --> 00:01:50,347
The data is Chinook, a free public sample database of a made-up music store.

24
00:01:50,367 --> 00:01:58,380
I wrote twenty English questions, and for each one a gold SQL answer, and I checked every gold answer by hand.

25
00:01:58,400 --> 00:02:08,752
For each question, the model sees the database schema and a strict contract: reply with exactly one SELECT statement, or the words cannot answer.

26
00:02:08,772 --> 00:02:18,480
CheckedSQL checks the contract, runs the query on a read-only copy with a five second limit, and compares the rows with the gold answer.

27
00:02:18,500 --> 00:02:19,949
Then it checks itself.

28
00:02:19,969 --> 00:02:24,222
It flags any gold answer that is an arbitrary pick between ties.

29
00:02:24,242 --> 00:02:30,299
That happened to me on October twenty-first: four years were tied at eighty-three invoices.

30
00:02:30,319 --> 00:02:35,440
And it runs a list of deliberately wrong answers, which should all be caught.

31
00:02:35,460 --> 00:02:37,011
Do I have what I need?

32
00:02:37,031 --> 00:02:40,509
The code is Python and SQLite, and it's all free.

33
00:02:40,529 --> 00:02:51,933
I don't have API credits yet, so this run used Claude Opus five point five inside Claude Code: twenty fresh copies, each seeing only the rules and one question.

34
00:02:51,953 --> 00:02:57,930
The API version is built and tested, and it runs with one command once I have a key.

35
00:02:57,950 --> 00:02:59,645
How will I know it worked?

36
00:02:59,665 --> 00:03:10,201
I measure three things: how many answers match the gold answer, how many replies keep the contract, and how many deliberately wrong answers the checker catches.

37
00:03:10,221 --> 00:03:12,905
It works if every wrong answer is caught.

38
00:03:12,925 --> 00:03:15,940
It fails if a wrong answer can score as right.

39
00:03:15,960 --> 00:03:17,325
Here's the real run.

40
00:03:17,345 --> 00:03:21,549
All twenty answers matched, and all twenty kept the contract.

41
00:03:21,569 --> 00:03:26,950
But a perfect score on twenty questions mostly tells me my questions are easy.

42
00:03:26,970 --> 00:03:31,773
The more useful result: the checker caught seven of eight wrong answers.

43
00:03:31,793 --> 00:03:36,529
The one it missed is question sixteen, revenue as price times quantity.

44
00:03:36,549 --> 00:03:42,491
Every quantity in this database is one, so a query that forgets it gets the same numbers.

45
00:03:42,511 --> 00:03:46,510
That's the false positive Zhong and colleagues warned about.

46
00:03:46,530 --> 00:03:53,513
Next, I'll add harder questions and test data where quantities vary, then run it on the API.

47
00:03:53,533 --> 00:03:59,450
CheckedSQL: not an AI that's always right, but one whose mistakes you can see.

48
00:03:59,470 --> 00:04:00,440
I'm Yuhao Fu.
